```java
class Cache {
    private final Map<String, String> data = new HashMap<>();
    private final Object lock = new Object();

    String get(String key) {
        synchronized (lock) {
            return data.get(key);
        }
    }

    void put(String key, String value) {
        synchronized (lock) {
            data.put(key, value);
        }
    }
}
```

Every `get`, even from ten threads that are only reading, serializes through the same lock as every `put` — two threads that both just want to read never need to conflict with each other, but this design forces them to anyway.

```java
class Cache {
    private final Map<String, String> data = new HashMap<>();
    private final ReadWriteLock rwLock = new ReentrantReadWriteLock();

    String get(String key) {
        rwLock.readLock().lock();
        try {
            return data.get(key);
        } finally {
            rwLock.readLock().unlock();
        }
    }

    void put(String key, String value) {
        rwLock.writeLock().lock();
        try {
            data.put(key, value);
        } finally {
            rwLock.writeLock().unlock();
        }
    }
}
```

Multiple threads calling `get` now hold the read lock together and proceed in parallel. A thread calling `put` waits for all current readers to finish, then holds the write lock exclusively — no reader or writer runs concurrently with it.
